    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p class="lead">Working from home sounds simple until you realize your manager can't see you, your teammates are in three time zones, and every misunderstanding happens in writing. The skills needed for remote work go beyond knowing your job. Employers want proof that you can communicate clearly, manage yourself and use digital tools safely. This guide covers 12 remote digital work skills and, just as important, how to prove each one on your résumé.</p>

<div class="key-takeaways"><p class="kt-title">Key takeaways</p><ul>
<li>Remote employers hire for communication, self-management and digital fluency as much as technical skill.</li>
<li>Clear writing is the single most valuable remote skill because most work happens in text.</li>
<li>Basic cybersecurity habits, like multi-factor authentication and spotting phishing, are now expected.</li>
<li>Prove every skill on your résumé with a tool, an action and a measurable result.</li>
<li>Match the exact skill words in each job post to get past applicant tracking systems.</li>
</ul></div>

<h2 id="why-remote-skills-are-different">Why remote work skills are different</h2>
<p>As the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Remote_work" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia overview of remote work</a> explains, remote and telecommuting arrangements rely on information technology and self-directed workflows instead of a shared office. That shift changes what "good at your job" looks like.</p>
<p>In an office, a manager can see you working, a quick question takes ten seconds, and a confused face gets noticed. Remotely, none of that happens automatically. You have to make your progress visible, ask good questions in writing and solve small problems on your own. Those habits are learnable, and they're exactly what hiring managers screen for.</p>

<h2 id="core-skills-needed-for-remote-work">The core skills needed for remote work</h2>
<p>Here are the 12 skills our recruiters at Best Jobs Pro see employers ask about most often in remote and hybrid job postings, grouped into communication, self-management, technical and people skills.</p>

<h3>1. Clear written communication</h3>
<p>Most remote work happens in email, chat, tickets and shared docs. Strong remote writers give context up front, state what they need and by when, and summarize decisions. A good test: could someone who missed the meeting act on your message without asking a follow-up question?</p>

<h3>2. Knowing when to go async vs live</h3>
<p>Asynchronous communication (messages people answer on their own time) respects time zones and focus time. Live calls are better for sensitive feedback, brainstorming or untangling a long thread. Knowing which to use, and saying "let's hop on a 10-minute call" when a chat goes in circles, is a real skill.</p>

<h3>3. Self-management and time management</h3>
<p>No one is walking past your desk. You need a system for setting priorities, planning your day and hitting deadlines. That might be a simple daily top-three list, time blocking on your calendar or a task app. The method matters less than doing it consistently.</p>

<h3>4. Accountability and visible progress</h3>
<p>Remote managers judge you by output and communication. Share short status updates before anyone asks, flag blockers early and follow through on what you said you'd do. Reliability becomes your reputation.</p>

<h3>5. Focus and workspace control</h3>
<p>Distractions at home look different from the office: family, chores, the TV, your phone. Remote pros set boundaries, protect deep-work blocks and keep a dedicated workspace, even if it's one corner of a kitchen table. Parents juggling this may find ideas in our post on <a href="/flexible-scheduling-for-parents-in-todays-workforce/">flexible scheduling for parents</a>.</p>

<h2 id="digital-literacy-skills">Digital literacy skills for remote digital work</h2>
<p>Digital literacy skills are the foundation of remote digital work. You don't need to be a programmer, but you do need to be comfortable in common tools and quick to learn new ones.</p>

<h3>6. Core collaboration tools</h3>
<p>Expect to use some combination of these every day:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Communication:</strong> Slack, Microsoft Teams, Google Chat, email</li>
<li><strong>Video:</strong> Zoom, Teams, Google Meet</li>
<li><strong>Documents:</strong> Google Workspace, Microsoft 365, Notion</li>
<li><strong>Project tracking:</strong> Asana, Trello, Monday.com, Jira</li>
<li><strong>File sharing:</strong> Google Drive, OneDrive, Dropbox, SharePoint</li>
</ul>

<h3>7. Basic tech troubleshooting</h3>
<p>When your microphone stops working five minutes before a call, there's no IT person down the hall. Know how to restart and update apps, check audio and video settings, test your internet speed and clear your browser cache. Escalate to IT with a clear description when the basics don't work.</p>

<h3>8. Cybersecurity awareness</h3>
<p>Working outside the office network puts more responsibility on you. The <a href="https://www.cisa.gov/cybersecurity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA)</a> recommends habits every remote worker should follow:</p>
<ul>
<li>Use strong, unique passwords and a password manager.</li>
<li>Turn on multi-factor authentication wherever it's offered.</li>
<li>Install software updates promptly.</li>
<li>Learn to recognize phishing emails and texts, and report them instead of clicking.</li>
<li>Avoid doing sensitive work on public Wi-Fi without a company VPN.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><p>Remote job scams are common. Real employers don't ask you to pay for equipment upfront, deposit a check and send money back, or share bank logins before you're hired. If an offer feels rushed or too good to be true, verify the company independently.</p></div>

<h2 id="soft-skills-for-remote-jobs">Soft skills for remote jobs that set you apart</h2>
<p>Soft skills for remote jobs often decide who gets hired between two candidates with similar experience.</p>

<h3>9. Problem-solving and initiative</h3>
<p>Remote teams value people who try to find the answer before asking. Search the shared docs, check past threads and test a fix. When you do ask, explain what you already tried. That saves everyone time and shows judgment.</p>

<h3>10. Collaboration across time zones and cultures</h3>
<p>Distributed teams work best with good documentation. Write things down, keep shared files organized and note your working hours in your status. Be patient with different communication styles and response times.</p>

<h3>11. Emotional intelligence</h3>
<p>Text strips out tone, so messages are easy to misread. Assume good intent, re-read before you hit send and use a quick call when a thread gets tense. Noticing when a teammate seems overwhelmed, and checking in, builds trust fast.</p>

<h3>12. Adaptability and continuous learning</h3>
<p>Tools and processes change often on remote teams. People who pick up new software quickly and keep learning on their own are the ones who get trusted with more. Free tool certifications and short online courses are easy ways to show this.</p>

<h2 id="prove-skills-on-your-resume">How to prove the skills needed for remote work on your résumé</h2>
<p>Listing "excellent communication skills" doesn't convince anyone. The strongest remote job skills for resume sections pair a skill with a tool, an action and a result. Use this table as a starting point.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Skill</th><th>How to prove it on a résumé</th><th>Example bullet</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Written communication</td><td>Mention documentation, reports or customer messages you wrote, with volume or impact</td><td>Wrote 30+ help-center articles that cut repeat support tickets</td></tr>
<tr><td>Async collaboration</td><td>Name the tools and the team size or time zones involved</td><td>Coordinated a six-person team across three time zones using Slack and Asana</td></tr>
<tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Show deadlines met or workload handled without supervision</td><td>Managed 40 client accounts independently with a 98% on-time delivery rate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accountability</td><td>Reference status reporting or ownership of outcomes</td><td>Sent weekly progress reports to leadership for a fully remote rollout</td></tr>
<tr><td>Digital literacy</td><td>List specific software in a skills section and in bullets</td><td>Migrated team files from shared drives to Microsoft 365 and SharePoint</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tech troubleshooting</td><td>Describe issues you resolved and who it helped</td><td>Served as go-to tech contact for a 12-person remote office</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cybersecurity awareness</td><td>Cite security training, MFA rollouts or policy compliance</td><td>Completed annual security awareness training; helped roll out MFA to staff</td></tr>
<tr><td>Problem-solving</td><td>Give a before-and-after result</td><td>Built a shared tracking sheet that reduced order errors by 25%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Adaptability</td><td>Show tools learned quickly or role changes handled</td><td>Learned Salesforce in two weeks to cover a vacant coordinator role</td></tr>
<tr><td>Emotional intelligence</td><td>Point to customer satisfaction, retention or mentoring</td><td>Maintained a 4.8/5 customer satisfaction score over 1,000+ chats</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Tip from our team:</strong> Only use numbers you can explain in an interview. A rough but honest figure ("about 50 tickets a day") is far better than an impressive one you can't back up.</p></div>

<h3>Match the job post's language</h3>
<p>Many employers screen résumés with applicant tracking software that looks for the exact skills named in the job post. If the listing says "Microsoft Teams" and "asynchronous communication," use those words, not just "video calls" and "messaging." Paste a job description and your résumé into the checker below to see which keywords you're missing.</p>

{{tool}}

